The only Filters option the AI Index records with "Incident" in its label is **My Open Incidents**.

According to the AI Index, the Filters menu on the Incidents list shows "Edit personal filters" and some preset filters: Active, Active - Unassigned, Assigned to me, Closed and My Open Incidents. Of these, only My Open Incidents contains "Incident". The index calls these presets "such as", so the menu may have other options that weren't recorded.

\boxed{My Open Incidents}

The largest SSD option for the developer laptop costs an extra $300.

In the portal, the Dell XPS 13 is listed as "Development Laptop (PC)." It comes with a 250 GB SSD at no extra cost. The only other drive option is 500 GB, which adds $300.00 to the $1,000.00 base price.

\boxed{300}

I couldn't find this in the knowledge base, so I don't know which field the sort row shows before you choose one.

I searched it several ways. It does describe sorting with the filter controls: open "Show / hide filter", click "Add Sort" to add an "Order results by the following fields" row, pick the field and the direction ("a to z" or "z to a"), then click "Run filter". It also lists the All Assets list columns (Asset tag, Display name, Model category, and so on). But no entry says which field the new sort row starts with, on the All Assets list or any other list.

\boxed{Unknown — not recorded in the knowledge base}

In the Catalog Items list view (sc_cat_item_list.do), the column between "Price" and "Delivery time" is **Class**. The portal notes in the AI Index list the default columns in this order:

Select All, Name, Short description, Catalogs, Category, Type, Price, **Class**, Delivery time, Description, Fulfillment automation level

\boxed{Class}

The checkbox directly above **Run at** is **Run business rules and engines**.

My source is the knowledge index for your ServiceNow portal. It lists the fields on the Data Management Delete Job form (`sys_dm_delete`) in this order: Condition, then "Run business rules and engines", then "Run at". It doesn't describe the exact screen layout, so "directly above" is based on that field order.

\boxed{Run business rules and engines}

On the incident form, the extra button at the top right is **Resolve**.

The portal notes say the Incident form has **Submit** and **Resolve** buttons at both the top and bottom of the form. The notes on the Change Request form only mention **Submit**, as the step that creates the request, and never list a Resolve button. So Resolve is the incident-only button, though the notes don't include a full list of the change-request form's buttons.

\boxed{Resolve}

\boxed{False — only the Incident form marks Short description as required}

- **Incident form:** Several notes in the knowledge index say Short description is mandatory, along with Caller. Both have to be filled in before you can submit.
- **Create Change Request form:** Short description is on the form and is listed as a field you fill in, but nothing in the index marks it as mandatory. The only special fields recorded for this form are the read-only ones: Type, Conflict status and Conflict last run. The index doesn't say outright that Short description is optional here, so "False" assumes that no mention of it being required means it isn't required.

On the `Data Management Delete Job` form (`sys_dm_delete`), two fields are read-only: **Number** and **State**. Two separate notes in the AI Index both say this.

For reference, the same notes say Table is mandatory. Condition, "Run business rules and engines" and "Run at" are listed as other fields on the form.

\boxed{Number, State}

